              {
                "text": "I'm trying to figure out the underlying rule by examining the layers from innermost to outermost and comparing them to the output rows, checking whether the count relates to the number of holes or gaps in each ring.\n\nChecking the second example's hole positions per ring, I get counts 3,2,3,1,0, which doesn't obviously match a layer count either. I'm now wondering if the number relates to how far the hole is from some reference point, or perhaps encodes a direction rather than a magnitude.\n\nTesting a direction hypothesis (down=3, left=2, up=1, right=0) against example 1 fails since a right-facing hole should map to 0 but seemed to give 3. Maybe the layer rows aren't ordered inner-to-outer, or maybe the count reflects something else entirely, like a step sequence tied to the exit direction as an arrow pattern. I'm re-checking example 3's inner-to-outer layer values against its output rows to see if a different mapping fits.\n\nTesting this against example 2 confirms left/down/up/right all match, but example 1 breaks the pattern\u2014the 4 ring hole points right which should mean 0, yet the actual output shows 3. This makes me reconsider whether the direction-to-number mapping or the ring ordering I assumed is actually correct.\n\nSo working through ex1 I get right=3, left=1, down=2, but ex3 gives down=3, right=0, left=2, up=1 -- these don't match up, which suggests there's an external orientation marker in each grid affecting the mapping. Ex1 has a marker at top, ex2 has one on the right side, ex3 has one on the right too, so maybe the output value depends on rotating the exit direction relative to that marker's position rather than being a fixed direction-to-number mapping.\n\nTesting this: counting counterclockwise steps from the marker direction to the exit direction matches all three examples consistently -- right marker gives right=0, up=1, left=2, down=3, and an up marker gives up=0, left=1, down=2, right=3. This confirms the rule, so now I need to apply it to the test input.\n\n",
